<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Matamba</h1><p class="st-meta">1550 CE – 1909 CE (approx.) · capital Santa Maria de Matamba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Mbundu kingdom east of Ndongo, often ruled by women, which Queen Njinga made the base of her long resistance to Portugal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Matamba lay in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwango-river">Kwango</a> valley and was a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a> in the early 16th century. It was ruled by queens in the later 16th century. Around 1631 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba">Njinga Mbande</a>, driven out of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo</a>, conquered Matamba and made it the centre of her state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba">Njinga</a> ruled with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbangala">Imbangala</a> warriors, trading captives to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> during their occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> and later to the Portuguese after the peace of 1656. She accepted Christian missionaries, and Santa Maria de Matamba became her capital. After her death in 1663 her sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbara-of-matamba">Barbara</a> and later rulers continued the line, and the kingdom served as a slave-trading intermediary between the interior and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Matamba remained independent through the 18th and 19th centuries, sometimes at war with Portugal or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kasanje">Kingdom of Kasanje</a>. Portuguese forces finally occupied it in 1909, and it was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1550 CE – 1909 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.55%;width:7.14%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1550 CE – 1909 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santa Maria de Matamba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Njinga Mbande, Barbara of Matamba, Veronica I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Mbundu religion, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kimbundu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Matamba, Ndongo-Matamba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=kingdom-of-matamba" aria-label="Kingdom of Matamba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5185 2625 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Matamba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5395 2668h0M5366 2764h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5505 2881h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5505 2881h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Angola. <a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=kingdom-of-matamba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

